00:00:03.189 --> 00:00:04.870 align:start position:0%
a historic move years in the making to  protect our wild horse and burrow  population not only in nevada but all  across the west the u.s senate  appropriations committee has earmarked  more than 10 million dollars for the blm  to use fertility control rather than  roundups madison kimbrough has the story  for decades the bureau of land  management has resisted controlling wild  horses with fertility and instead using  force the helicopter roundups are  horrifically cruel these animals suffer  significantly during the process wild  horse advocates like gann vice are  hopeful that that could change now that  there is an appropriations bill  providing 11 million dollars for  fertility control it could help move  away from the current roundup program  that lands thousands of horses into  holding pins it's very traumatic for the  horses  many of them suffer from broken necks  broken legs to hold the agency  accountable blm must report back within  45 days with its strategy for a  vaccination initiative in a statement  the agency said the blm makes every  effort to conduct gathers in a safe and  humane manner every attempt is made to  place them into good private homes but  no comment on the current initiative  which was just announced fertility  control vaccine  provides a humane alternative that is  also very cost effective  to allow these animals to stay in the  wild where they belong madison kimbrough  live local now  blm's current management plan is to  remove 90 thousand horses and burros  from public lands by the year 2025.
 

